From f34d746c99986cefb9c2edda2e37fe47252d797e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: leow149 <leow149@lucidmail.xyz>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 14:30:30 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 001/100] mmc: pwrseq_simple: Fall back to GPIOs when no reset
 controller can be made

Since pwrseq_simple asks the reset core for a reset control when there is
a single reset GPIO, the probe fails on boards whose GPIO controller does
not use two-cell specifiers: the reset-gpio helper only handles
two-cell GPIO specifiers and returns -ENOENT for anything else. All
Allwinner pin controllers use three cells, and arm64 defconfig has
CONFIG_RESET_GPIO=m, so an mmc-pwrseq-simple with one reset GPIO, the
usual SDIO Wi-Fi setup, no longer probes on them.

Treat -ENOENT as "no reset controller" and drive the GPIO directly, as the
driver does without CONFIG_RESET_GPIO. With a single reset GPIO and
CONFIG_RESET_GPIO enabled, -ENOENT only comes from
__reset_add_reset_gpio_device() refusing a specifier that is not two
cells; a missing property or CONFIG_RESET_GPIO=n already give NULL.

Reset maintainers: would you rather have reset-gpio accept any number
of GPIO cells, by passing the GPIO's own specifier through, so that this
consumer-side fallback is not needed?

Fixes: 73bf4b7381f7 ("mmc: pwrseq_simple: add support for one reset control")
Cc: stable@vger.kernel.org
Assisted-by: Claude Opus 5.5
Signed-off-by: leow149 <leow149@lucidmail.xyz>
---
 drivers/mmc/core/pwrseq_simple.c | 8 +++++++-
 1 file changed, 7 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/mmc/core/pwrseq_simple.c b/drivers/mmc/core/pwrseq_simple.c
index 4b47e6c3b..0e25fc957 100644
--- a/drivers/mmc/core/pwrseq_simple.c
+++ b/drivers/mmc/core/pwrseq_simple.c
@@ -137,7 +137,13 @@ static int mmc_pwrseq_simple_probe(struct platform_device *pdev)
 	ngpio = of_count_phandle_with_args(dev->of_node, "reset-gpios", "#gpio-cells");
 	if (ngpio == 1) {
 		pwrseq->reset_ctrl = devm_reset_control_get_optional_shared(dev, NULL);
-		if (IS_ERR(pwrseq->reset_ctrl))
+		/*
+		 * -ENOENT: the GPIO cannot be wrapped as a reset controller
+		 * (e.g. #gpio-cells = <3>), drive it directly below.
+		 */
+		if (PTR_ERR(pwrseq->reset_ctrl) == -ENOENT)
+			pwrseq->reset_ctrl = NULL;
+		else if (IS_ERR(pwrseq->reset_ctrl))
 			return dev_err_probe(dev, PTR_ERR(pwrseq->reset_ctrl),
 					     "reset control not ready\n");
 	}
